WEBVTT

3.1.1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Changing the harness can change a model’s results; controlled comparisons show why.

3.1.2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:18.425
In the peer-reviewed SWE-agent study, GPT-4 Turbo rose from 11% to 18% on SWE-bench Lite, with demonstrations supplied to both systems.

3.1.3
00:00:18.725 --> 00:00:25.400
Separate ablations tested linting, shorter observation history, windowed viewing, and summarized search.

3.1.4
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:32.400
These are configuration-specific comparisons, not independent gains that can be added together.

3.1.5
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.650
Holding the model and tasks fixed makes the interface easier to study.

3.2.1
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:50.175
Peer-reviewed CodeAct found executable Python beating JSON actions, 74.4% against 52.4%, on composed tool tasks.

3.2.2
00:00:50.475 --> 00:00:53.775
But code could compose more work inside each turn.

3.2.3
00:00:54.075 --> 00:01:00.600
On atomic calls, JSON won instead: the action space mattered, not just serialization.

3.3.1
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:09.075
Peer-reviewed Agentless demonstrated a competitive prescribed workflow, rather than unrestricted agent control.

3.3.2
00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:19.700
In peer-reviewed HAL, GPT-4.1 rose from 2% to 44% after a scaffold swap, at substantially higher cost.

3.3.3
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.325
These are best-run whole-system results, not average gains from a single tool.

3.3.4
00:01:25.625 --> 00:01:34.325
Terminal-Bench likewise found minimal scaffolds competitive, while Codex CLI remained stronger for the GPT models tested.

3.3.5
00:01:34.625 --> 00:01:39.450
HAL’s second comparison also traded higher cost for higher success.

3.4.1
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:49.100
Context is another harness decision: the peer-reviewed Lost in the Middle study found retrieval sensitive to information position.

3.4.2
00:01:49.400 --> 00:02:00.675
Dynamic Cheatsheet took GPT-4o from 19% to 99% with curated memory, but on a repetitive arithmetic task, Game of 24.

3.4.3
00:02:00.975 --> 00:02:07.375
ACE improved AppWorld by adapting context offline, using labeled training examples.

3.4.4
00:02:07.675 --> 00:02:14.225
The lesson is to select useful evidence, rather than always shrink or always retain context.

3.5.1
00:02:15.325 --> 00:02:23.250
Peer-reviewed ADAS, AFlow, and Darwin Gödel Machine automate harness design while keeping execution models frozen.

3.5.2
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:34.175
Darwin Gödel Machine transferred an evolved agent to unseen Polyglot tasks, improving from 14.2% to 28.9%.

3.5.3
00:02:34.475 --> 00:02:43.825
Peer-reviewed GEPA made prompt optimization competitive with reinforcement learning, though reinforcement learning won its AIME evaluation.

3.5.4
00:02:44.125 --> 00:02:51.150
The Meta-Harness preprint also searched coding harnesses, but reused the evaluation tasks during search.

3.5.5
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:56.175
Those searches still consume compute, and may use stronger helper models.

3.6.1
00:02:57.275 --> 00:03:04.925
Now, first-party reports: Chroma found focused excerpts better than full history, with different retrieval burdens.

3.6.2
00:03:05.225 --> 00:03:14.650
LangChain reports 52.8% to 66.5% on Terminal-Bench, without a held-out generalization test.

3.6.3
00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:27.900
Can Bölük reports Grok Code Fast 1 rising from 6.7% to 68.3% with hashline, on synthetic React mutations checked against original files.

3.6.4
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:34.200
These are engineering leads, but their gains need testing on ordinary repository work.

3.7.1
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:45.700
Diff-XYZ’s revised preprint found search-and-replace helping GPT-4.1 but hurting its nano counterpart in diff generation.

3.7.2
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.050
More instructions, memory, and reasoning can also hurt, depending on the task and feedback.

3.7.3
00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:57.550
A larger model’s preferred interface may be a poor default for a smaller one.

3.8.1
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:04.625
OpenAI documents training for its patch format; compatibility is part of the story.

3.8.2
00:04:04.925 --> 00:04:10.600
DeepSeek’s technical report goes further, describing training across several harnesses.

3.8.3
00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:16.325
The evidence supports measuring harness design, not crowning a universal winner.
